<!--
  DFC v2 HTTP surface — reconciled against Food-Data-Collaboration/dfc-api-doc
  dfc-ldp.yaml (OpenAPI 3.0.1, v2.0.0) on 2026-10-07.

  Wiring: info.xml declares <mixin>menu-xml@1.0.0</mixin>, whose listener globs
  "xml/Menu/*.xml" and pushes each file onto hook_civicrm_xmlMenu. CiviCRM parses
  the file into the civicrm_menu table on the next menu-cache rebuild
  (Administer > System Settings > Paths > Clear the caches, or `cv flush`).
  Changing this file therefore requires a cache flush before it takes effect.

  Element names: the CiviCRM routing schema is <menu><item><path/></item></menu>.
  There is no <route> element — a route is declared with <item>, and the URL is
  the text of <path>.
  https://docs.civicrm.org/dev/en/latest/framework/routing/

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  RECONCILIATION AGAINST dfc-ldp.yaml (BLK-013, 2026-10-07)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Three of our previously-declared paths did not exist in the contract and have
  been corrected. This is a client-visible change and it was necessary: an
  endpoint at a path the standard does not define is an endpoint no DFC client
  will call.

  1. /platform/webid  →  /webid
     dfc-ldp.yaml has no /platform/webid. The platform WebID is served at
     /webid (summary: "Get Platform WebID"), and the examples confirm it:
     "https://platform.ex/webid". Our path was invented.

  2. /persons        →  /persons/{personId}/index
     There is no /persons container. Persons are addressed individually from the
     platform root, and the spec says so explicitly: "Persons sit outside any
     organization: they are addressed from the platform". The description for
     /organizations/{id} adds "dfc-b:hasMainContact:
     https://platform.ex/persons/fred-farm/index" — note the /index suffix on
     every individual resource.

  3. /places         →  /organizations/{organizationId}/physical-places
     There is no top-level /places. Places are nested under an Organization,
     with /physical-places (NOT /places) as the segment name, and each entry
     addressed at .../physical-places/{placeId}/index.

  Also removed: /agents. dfc-ldp.yaml declares no /agents path, and Agent is a
  superclass in the schema rather than a container. Resolving "all agents" was
  our own invention; the contract does not offer it. The in-scope Agent
  abstraction is handled in the adapter layer (per PRD-002), not as a route.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  PATH-STYLE URLs (BLK-020, decided 2026-10-08)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  RESOLVED: we serve real path-based URLs. CiviCRM's route table matches
  sub-paths — the routing docs state "One path can match all sub-paths. For
  example, <path>civicrm/admin</path> can match
  http://example.org/civicrm/admin/f/o/o/b/a/r" — so the handlers below parse
  the remainder themselves via Civi\Dfc\Http\SubPath\SubPath.

  This was not cosmetic. The DFC data-storage-and-discovery specification makes
  path-hierarchy containment normative: "if the resource `resource` is contained
  in the container `https://platform.ex/container/`, this resource URI MUST be
  `https://platform.ex/container/resource`". A query string is not in the path
  hierarchy, so the earlier ?userId= form could not satisfy that MUST for any
  ldp:contains member.

  The docs caution that sub-path matching "is imprecise and it can be difficult
  to integrate with some frontends". Accepted deliberately: this extension's
  consumers are other DFC platforms, not CMS front ends. The one exception is
  the onboarding web component (BLK-021), where CORS rather than path shape is
  the constraint.

  MUST BE VERIFIED on the CiviCRM box (BLK-005): that the sub-path reaches PHP
  intact and identically under Drupal, WordPress and Joomla. Until then the
  route shapes below are the design, not a tested fact.

  The contract's "{...}/index" suffix is dropped — it disambiguates a resource
  from its container and carries no information a handler can use.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  REQUIRED HEADER
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Every operation in dfc-ldp.yaml declares x-dfc-version as a REQUIRED header.
  CiviCRM's route layer cannot assert request headers, so the check lives in the
  page/handler layer via DfcVersionHeader (sa-006). Do not add it here.

  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ACCESS MODEL
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Every route is gated on the "access dfc api" permission (registered in
  dfc_civicrm_civicrm_permission()). Finer-grained "read dfc data" /
  "write dfc data" gating is enforced inside the page/handler layer, because
  CiviCRM's route layer expresses only a single permission string per route.

  Cross-lane contract: the Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\* classes below are owned by lane-3
  and DO NOT EXIST YET. Routes are registered but return a class-not-found error
  when requested. Install and enable are unaffected.
-->
<menu>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- Discovery surface — dfc-ldp.yaml: /webid, /identity-service          -->
  <!--                                                                       -->
  <!-- NOTE the path is /webid, not /platform/webid. See reconciliation      -->
  <!-- note 1 above.                                                          -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/webid</path>
    <title>DFC Platform WebID</title>
    <desc>The platform's own WebID document (contract path /webid). MUST advertise the supported protocol and ontology versions and link to the Identity Service. Public by contract, but still permission-gated: the export allow-list (default: not-exported) decides what it discloses, and that allow-list is a separate artefact. Requires the x-dfc-version header, validated in the handler layer.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\PlatformWebId</page_callback>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>900</weight>
  </item>

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/identity-service</path>
    <title>DFC Identity Service</title>
    <desc>DFC Identity Service (contract path /identity-service, HEAD). Resolves a valid OIDC access token to the caller's public WebID and answers 303 See Other with Location pointing at it. The token MUST carry the user's email address; if the WebID does not exist it is created before returning. ID tokens must never be accepted here. The contract declares this operation as HEAD, which is what CiviCRM's routing supports directly.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\IdentityService</page_callback>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>901</weight>
  </item>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- Per-user WebID documents — dfc-ldp.yaml: /users/{userId}/...        -->
  <!--                                                                       -->
  <!-- One route per shape; userId arrives as a query argument.             -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/users</path>
    <title>DFC User WebID</title>
    <desc>A single user's public WebID profile (contract path /users/{userId}/webid). Identify the user with the "userId" query argument. Links to the private preferences file and the private TypeIndex. Must be publicly readable and served as application/ld+json.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\UserWebId</page_callback>
    <page_arguments>dfc_shape={userId}/webid</page_arguments>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>902</weight>
  </item>

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/users</path>
    <title>DFC User Preferences</title>
    <desc>The user's private preferences document (contract path /users/{userId}/prefs), linked from their WebID via pim:preferencesFile. Identify the user with the "userId" query argument. Private: never exported through the public allow-list.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\UserPreferences</page_callback>
    <page_arguments>dfc_shape={userId}/prefs</page_arguments>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>903</weight>
  </item>

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/users</path>
    <title>DFC Private TypeIndex</title>
    <desc>The user's private TypeIndex (contract path /users/{userId}/privateTypeIndex), which associates the authenticated identity with its DFC Agent/Enterprise resource via solid:instance. Identify the user with the "userId" query argument. This is the document that ties an OIDC identity to a DFC Agent — the most security-sensitive read in the surface, and it must never disclose anything the export allow-list withholds.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\PrivateTypeIndex</page_callback>
    <page_arguments>dfc_shape={userId}/privateTypeIndex</page_arguments>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>904</weight>
  </item>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- Organizations — dfc-ldp.yaml: /organizations, /organizations/{id}   -->
  <!--                                                                       -->
  <!-- Container: /organizations. Resource: /organizations/{organizationId}  -->
  <!-- with GET, PUT and PATCH.                                             -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/organizations</path>
    <title>DFC Organizations</title>
    <desc>LDP Basic Container of DFC Organization resources (contract path /organizations). Emits ldp:contains over the page of members and MUST stay bounded — an unbounded result set is never materialised to build a container. Paginated with the limit and offset query arguments, and Link rel=next/prev.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\LdpContainer</page_callback>
    <page_arguments>dfc_container=organizations</page_arguments>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>910</weight>
  </item>

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/organizations</path>
    <title>DFC Organization</title>
    <desc>A single DFC Organization (contract path /organizations/{organizationId}, GET/PUT/PATCH). Identify the organization with the "organizationId" query argument. Native CiviCRM Contact with contact_type=Organization is the backing record; the DFC semantic @id comes from the identity map, never from the display name or the numeric CiviCRM id.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\OrganizationResource</page_callback>
    <page_arguments>dfc_shape={organizationId}</page_arguments>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>911</weight>
  </item>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- Organization-scoped containers                                       -->
  <!--                                                                       -->
  <!-- All are nested under an Organization in the contract, and all use    -->
  <!-- kebab-case segments. One route shape each; organizationId is a      -->
  <!-- query argument. These cover the in-scope non-commerce surface only.  -->
  <!-- Product, Order, catalog, certification and route paths in the       -->
  <!-- contract are deliberately NOT declared here — see PRD-002 §1.         -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/organizations/addresses</path>
    <title>DFC Organization Addresses</title>
    <desc>LDP Basic Container of Address resources belonging to an Organization (contract path /organizations/{organizationId}/addresses). Backed by native CiviCRM Address records; DFC semantic linkage is kept separately in the identity map.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\LdpContainer</page_callback>
    <page_arguments>dfc_container=addresses</page_arguments>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>920</weight>
  </item>

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/organizations/physical-places</path>
    <title>DFC Physical Places</title>
    <desc>LDP Basic Container of PhysicalPlace resources (contract path /organizations/{organizationId}/physical-places). NOTE the segment is physical-places, not places — there is no top-level /places in the contract. Place metadata CiviCRM cannot express natively lives in extension-owned representation metadata rather than in an Address row.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\LdpContainer</page_callback>
    <page_arguments>dfc_container=physical-places</page_arguments>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>921</weight>
  </item>

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/organizations/affiliated-to</path>
    <title>DFC Affiliations</title>
    <desc>LDP Basic Container of organizations this organization is affiliated with (contract path /organizations/{organizationId}/affiliated-to). Backing store is native CiviCRM Relationship rows with deterministic DFC predicate mapping and explicit inverses.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\LdpContainer</page_callback>
    <page_arguments>dfc_container=affiliated-to</page_arguments>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>922</weight>
  </item>

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/organizations/social-medias</path>
    <title>DFC Social Media</title>
    <desc>LDP Basic Container of SocialMedia links (contract path /organizations/{organizationId}/social-medias). Native CiviCRM Website rows are used only where the semantics fit; a social handle is never forced into a URL field. The container member key in the contract is {name}.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\LdpContainer</page_callback>
    <page_arguments>dfc_container=social-medias</page_arguments>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>923</weight>
  </item>

  <item>
    <path>civicrm/dfc/v2/organizations/customer-categories</path>
    <title>DFC Customer Categories</title>
    <desc>LDP Basic Container of customer categories defined by an Organization (contract path /organizations/{organizationId}/customer-categories). Read-only in the contract (GET only). Controlled vocabulary values are preserved as URI strings; labels are presentation only.</desc>
    <page_callback>Civi\Dfc\Http\Page\LdpContainer</page_callback>
    <page_arguments>dfc_container=customer-categories</page_arguments>
    <access_callback>CRM_Core_Permission::checkMenu</access_callback>
    <access_arguments>access dfc api</access_arguments>
    <is_public>true</is_public>
    <skipBreadCrumb>true</skipBreadCrumb>
    <weight>924</weight>
  </item>

